  "id": "COMPOUND:14825 - PROTEIN:83",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that tigecycline may interact with the Glucagon receptor through a series of molecular pathways. The drug is predicted to target Chloramphenicol acetyltransferase, which is in turn targeted by multiple compounds, including 18,19-Dihydroangustine and 2-[(3S)-3-hydroxy-2-oxo-1H-indol-3-yl]acetic acid. These compounds are all predicted to have a target of the Glucagon receptor, indicating a potential mechanism of action involving the Glucagon receptor as a downstream effector. The scores from the different paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, with some evidence pointing to a more direct interaction through the acetyltransferase pathway. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for the Glucagon receptor in the downstream effects of tigecycline, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact mechanism and the extent of the drug's influence on this receptor.",
